# Paths the factory refuses agent changes to, one glob per line. Blank lines and
# comment lines are ignored. "Protected" means a human reads this diff, not that
# the file can never change: a branch that has to touch one of these still lands,
# it just cannot be approved and closed without somebody looking. Adding a gate
# to bin/test is expected work and will show up here on purpose.
#
# .factory/gate and .factory/protected are always protected by the factory
# itself. The list below exists because .factory/gate only delegates, so
# protecting the wrapper alone protects nothing.
#
# Factory reads each line as a git pathspec, so a * matches across directories
# too. With :(glob), a * stays within one directory, **/ matches any directories
# or none, and a name covers only itself, so a directory that could be a link
# takes one line for its name and one for what is under it.

# The gate body. bin/test is the whole gate list and the one CI runs; the other
# four are the scripts it delegates to. Deleting a gate here, or a fixture
# beside it, passes every remaining check in this tree - which is exactly the
# move that has to be seen rather than inferred.
bin/test
bin/check-fast
bin/check-qml-contract
bin/check-gates
bin/check-worktree-hook

# The allowlist that excuses a gate from having a fixture. bin/check-fast reads
# it in both directions, so it cannot silently fill up - but adding a name to it
# is still how a gate stops being proven.
tests/gates/uncovered

# SHA-pinned CI and release automation. A workflow holds this repository's
# contents, and a mutable action reference hands that to whoever owns the action.
.github/workflows/*.yml

# cargo-deny's advisory policy, enforced by .github/workflows/audit.yml.
# Relaxing an entry here is a suppression wearing a config file's name.
deny.toml

# The justfile, in any directory and any spelling just reads, and the modules a
# justfile can load. Nothing in the gate runs its recipes, but a person does,
# `just install` among them, which runs sudo: a recipe an agent changed would run
# with that person's password behind it.
:(icase)*justfile
*.just

# The files that steer the pre-commit hook every worktree shares: they decide
# what that hook runs, and whether a commit in a worktree, or `mise install`,
# rewrites it for every checkout. lefthook.yml's no_auto_install keeps a commit
# from reinstalling the hook, and mise.toml's postinstall runs lefthook install
# only in the main checkout. A branch that drops either, or adds a
# lefthook-local file that turns no_auto_install off, rewrites the hook from
# its own worktree at its next commit or `mise install`, before Factory sees
# the branch; `mise install` in the main checkout writes it back.
#
# mise's config files, in any directory, since mise reads those of the
# directory a command runs in and of every one above it: mise.toml, .mise.toml
# and their local and per-environment forms, which **/ also finds under
# .config/; the config files under mise/, .mise/ and .config/mise/;
# .tool-versions; and a .miserc.toml, which can rename them. mise and .mise are
# listed alone too, since a link of either name would hand mise a config file
# from anywhere.
:(glob)**/mise*.toml
:(glob)**/.mise*.toml
:(glob)**/mise
:(glob)**/mise/**
:(glob)**/.mise
:(glob)**/.mise/**
:(glob)**/.tool-versions
# lefthook's, at the root: lefthook.yml, or the first of its other names, there
# or in .config/; a lefthook-local file, which lefthook merges over it; and the
# scripts a config names from .lefthook/.
lefthook*
.lefthook*
.config/lefthook*
# And .config itself, in any directory, as a name alone: a .config that is a
# link hands mise, wherever a command runs, and lefthook, at the root, config
# files from anywhere. Under a real .config, the files they read are covered
# above.
:(glob)**/.config
# And the root .gitignore, whose re-includes keep a global ignore from hiding
# a local one of these files, such as mise.local.toml or lefthook-local.yml: an
# ignored file is in no diff and in no status, so neither this list nor
# Factory's check for uncommitted changes would see it.
.gitignore
